Here is a simple, plain-English explanation of what localStorage means:
What is localStorage?
Think of localStorage as a small private notebook inside your web browser (Google Chrome, Safari, Edge, etc.) on your laptop or phone.
When you do things in the Calamansi app:
You log in as admin
You add a farmer user
You save a harvest calculation log
The website simply writes those notes into your browser’s local memory on that specific device.
Why 95% of College Capstone Projects Use It
For a thesis defense or student presentation, localStorage is actually the most popular and practical choice:
It’s 100% Free: You don’t need to pay for a monthly cloud database subscription (like Amazon AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure).
Zero Setup / Zero Maintenance: There are no database servers that can crash, expire, or run out of memory during your defense.
Works Even with Bad Wi-Fi: If the school or university Wi-Fi is slow or disconnects while presenting to your panelists, your app still works smoothly because the data is already stored inside your browser.
Immediate Testing: A teacher or panelist can open the app on a laptop and immediately test estimating juice without waiting for an SMS code or email confirmation link.
What Was the Reviewer Pointing Out? (The Limitations)
The reviewer was looking at it from the perspective of an enterprise company (like Facebook, GCash, or a commercial bank):
It Stays on One Machine:
If you log in and save a harvest on your laptop, and your classmate opens the link on their smartphone, they won’t see your saved harvest. Each browser has its own separate "notebook."
Clearing Browser History Resets It:
If you go to Chrome settings and click "Clear browsing data & cookies," the browser erases its local notebook, and the app resets back to its default demo users (admin / user).
Not Bank-Grade Password Security:
Passwords aren't encrypted with high-end banking hashes. A programmer who opens Chrome's Developer Tools (F12) can read what’s written in the browser's storage.
Does This Hurt Your Thesis Defense?
No, absolutely not.
In academic thesis defenses, panelists understand the difference between:
A Research Prototype / Proof-of-Concept (which is what your capstone is: proving that mathematical modeling can accurately predict calamansi juice yield).
A Commercial Enterprise SaaS (which requires monthly database hosting, cybersecurity compliance, and IT staff).
If a panelist ever asks: "Where is the data stored?"
You have a ready, professional answer:
"For this prototype, records and authentication are managed using browser-level local storage to keep the system lightweight, offline-capable, and cost-free for local farmers. For full commercial deployment across the province, the architecture is ready to connect to a centralized cloud database like PostgreSQL or Firebase."
